Developmentally, the diaphragm explains its own innervation. It forms from four sources:
- the **septum transversum**, giving the central tendon;
- the **pleuroperitoneal membranes**;
- the **dorsal mesentery of the esophagus**, giving the crura;
- body-wall mesoderm at the periphery.

Myoblasts from cervical somites migrate along the pleuroperitoneal folds. The septum transversum starts at cervical levels and later descends, dragging the C3–C5 phrenic nerves through the thorax. This is why a thoracoabdominal muscle is supplied by cervical nerves. Work by Merrell and Kardon showed that pleuroperitoneal-fold connective tissue directs this muscularization. Defects in the posterolateral pleuroperitoneal region produce **Bochdalek congenital diaphragmatic hernia** (mostly left-sided). Its lethality comes mainly from **pulmonary hypoplasia and pulmonary hypertension**, not the hernia itself.

Physiologically, the costal and crural parts behave differently. Costal fibers insert on the ribs. Through the **zone of apposition**, where the diaphragm lies against the inner rib cage, they lift and expand the lower ribs via abdominal pressure transmission (De Troyer's analyses). Crural fibers lack rib insertions, have a partly distinct motor-neuron pool, and relax during swallowing and vomiting. This supports the concept of a **two-sphincter gastroesophageal barrier**: an intrinsic lower esophageal sphincter plus an extrinsic crural sphincter.

The central drive comes from **inspiratory rhythm generation in the preBötzinger complex**. It relays through bulbospinal premotor neurons to the phrenic motor nucleus in the C3–C5 ventral horn. The diaphragm is composed mainly of fatigue-resistant fibers. Accessory phrenic nerves (often from C5 via the nerve to subclavius) occur in a substantial minority of people. The phrenic nerve divides at the diaphragm into roughly sternal, anterolateral, posterolateral and crural branches. That branching pattern guides **radial incisions** placed to avoid denervation.

The aortic hiatus is not true muscle, so **median arcuate ligament syndrome**, in which the celiac trunk is compressed, remains controversial. Is the pain from vascular ischemia or from irritation of the celiac ganglion? This is still debated.
